1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/126488085/lovese-g.-bakke
2 CONT
2 CONT With the passing of Mrs. Hans Bakke Maddock lost one of its most faithful church workers and one of its most respected citizens. She will be greatly missed by her neighbors and friends, because of her helpfulness and friendliness 
2 CONC at all times and in her home is a vacant place which can never be filled. Mrs. Lovise Bakke was born on 26 Feb 1869 in Ytre Holmedal, Sondfjord, Norway of parents Mrs. and Mrs. Christian Nelson Lone and there she was baptized and 
2 CONC confirmed by Rev. Schmit. On 14 Jun 1888 she was united in marriage to Hans Bakke at the Ytre Holmedal Church, with the same minister officiating. In 1890 Mr. and Mrs. Bakke emigrated to America and arrived at St. James, MN where 
2 CONC they resided for 2 years and then moved to Minneapolis and made their home for 5 years. In the spring of 1897 they followed the stream of homestead seekers to the west and settled 1 1/2 miles northwest of Maddock where Mr. Bakke b
2 CONC uilt a house and blacksmith shop. When the railroad came through, 4 years later, they moved their property to the newly plotted townsite, where they became the first residents of Maddock and Mr. Bakke continued at his trade as a b
2 CONC lacksmith. The first child born in the city of Maddock was their son, Arthur Bakke and here they joined the North Viking Lutheran Church and later Mrs. Bakke became a member of the Ladies Aid where she was a willing worker as long
2 CONC  as her health and strength permitted. Like the rest of the early settlers they faced a lot of hardships, but they always managed to make a good living and their home was a friendly meeting place for many Norwegians who frequently
2 CONC  spent the evening there reminiscing on the country they had left and which they could not forget. Ten years ago Mrs. Bakke underwent a serious operation and since that time her health has been poor but she attended to her househo
2 CONC ld duties until this fall when her condition became grave and she was confined to bed. Last summer their friends honored them on their golden wedding anniversary, the party being held at the church parlors and this was the last ti
2 CONC me she was able to come to church. The last few weeks she suffered a great deal, but she carried her burden patiently, trusting in her Lord and Savior until she passed away at home on Saturday, 12 Nov 1938 at 5:30 p.m. Funeral ser
2 CONC vices were conducted on Wednesday, 16 Nov 1938 at the North Viking Lutheran Church with Rev. G. R. Estrem officiating. A large crowd gathered to attend the last rites for the beloved departed and there were many floral offerings a
2 CONC nd memorial gifts to charities and mission. Pall bearers were G. C. Olson, J. H. Johnson, James Cleveland, Albert Hesla, Peter Dalbak and N. O. Halvorson. Interment was made in the North Viking Cemetery. She leaves to mourn her pa
2 CONC ssing her husband, Hans, son, Arthur and a daughter, Clara Mrs. G. S. Stenerodden. Five grandchildren also survive. A brother Abraham Lone and sisters Gurine and Thea all of whom reside in Sondfjord, Norway. She died trusting in h
2 CONC er Lord and Savior. God bless her memory.
