1 OCCU Factory Owner
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hans Krohn Magnus started together with his brother Jacob Magnus, a store in Bergen selling "Hvidevarer" or white goods in Bergen at "Torget". Prior to this he was one year with Robsahm in Oslo as a volunteer. For 
2 CONC this he got an umbrella as a salary. In 1898 there was a exhibition "landutstilling" and the two brothers were encouraged to start a production business. They investigated the import tarriff for various goods and tried to find som
2 CONC ething that was not being produced in Norway and also had high protection through the import tariffs. The first started with knitted gloves. It was said that they gave up this because they couldn't get the thumb right. Thereafter 
2 CONC they tried lace and this went much better.They registered the company Bergens Blondevæveri Magnus & Co on 25 September 1901. In 1904 Jacob Magnus stepped out of the company and ran the business in Bergen at "Torget"  on his own. T
2 CONC his store burnt down in the city fire in 1917.  The lace factory was first established at "Nøstet" in Bergen and later moved to "Skivebakken" in Bergen in 1906. This house still exists (1995) but is now converted to apartments. In
2 CONC  1927 a new building was built at Midttun at Nesttun in Bergen and the factory moved in to this new building. This building is owned by his grandson Hans Petter Magnus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans Magnus was married in 1899 to Agnes Nyblin Janson. She was the widow of Karl Nyblin whom died half a year after they were married. They had started a photography business together after having studied in Stockholm, Sweden. Th
2 CONC e Daughter Karla Nyblin was adopted by Hans Magnus. Hans and Agnes lived in Rosenberg Gaten 22, Bergen, not so far from his parents whom lived at Museplass. Later the couple moved to Nubbebakken and in 1915 Hans bought a small far
2 CONC m at Hopsneset, Bergen where he in 1917 built Hasselbakken. Agnes ran the photoshop and made enough money to take care of house expenses because the factory did not yield a profit in the beginning of their marriage. Their children
2 CONC  Birger and Karla was taken care of their maid "Anne Dadda". 
2 CONT
2 CONT Later Hans made speculative investment in shipping that made him a lot of money. This is the money that paid for the large house at Hopsneset. Hans had several friends that also built at Hop which at that time was a fairly rural a
2 CONC rea and little developed. Some of his friends was Shipowner Hans Westfal-Larsen,  Peter Petersen, Edvard Risøen and  Hans Bull Øvrevik.  His club friends was also Jacob Neumann, Harald  Mowinkel, Wald Jacobsen, Jacob Neumann.  Jac
2 CONC ob Neumann and Hans Westfal-Larsen was also at a time board members in the factory. Hans was a freemason. Hans and Agnes traveled on a cruise with Westfal-Larsen's ships. When they came onboard Mr. Westfal Larsen asked how much mo
2 CONC ney Hans had and asked if he could borrow the money for a while. When he got the money said that: "From now on you are not going to pay for anything, you are my guests". At the time Westfal-Larsen had Norway's second largest ship 
2 CONC owning business.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans was considered by his friends to be a very handsome man. (You will see that his great grandson Johan Gustav Hvide (b. 1959) has very much the same look as his great grandfather. Some people called him "Glansbildet". En verksm
2 CONC esteri konfeksjonsindustrien told his grandson, Hans Petter Magnus, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans Magnus var dyslektisk. 
