1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Evidently she had a “weak chest“, asthmatic, and when she finished school Dolly went to stay with relatives in Lillehammer, Norway, as her parents thought that staying in a cleaner atmosphere and a bracing climate 
2 CONC would be helpful. (Buffalo was an industrial city).
2 CONT
2 CONT Her family has a leather-bound notebook in which she has written an account of the trip to Norway and which is crammed with recipes which she found in Norway and later in Germany. This diary is copied without changes to punctuatio
2 CONC n, grammar or spelling. One is grateful that Dorothea’s handwriting is so clear.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Dairy of an American girl’s trip to Scandinavia'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Oct. 10, 1907 You have all read of friends waving handkerchiefs disappearing in the distance as the ocean liner wends its way out of harbor and no doubt you yourself have experienced belng among those on board waving your farewell
2 CONC  to loved ones. On this glorious fall day, the ozone and sunlight of which made one feel invigorated, happy and thankful for life, I left New York bound for Christiana on the Scandinavian-American Line. In my stateroom I was greet
2 CONC ed by a lovely bouquet of pink and white carnations (sent by Dr Lattin) and not least a gorgeous basket of fruit topped off by a huge cluster of Tokay grapes, one of those clusters which are used as models for the electric grape c
2 CONC lusters used in wonderful electric candelabras. I looked at my pink carnations and they reminded me of the chief reason for which I was taking my journey for health. They stood out for the pink glow of robustness and bodily roundn
2 CONC ess. Then the white flowers were a message of love, love next to love for God, love of parents for their children and it was this love which was sending me on a delightful journey. Statue of Liberty has disappeared and the last la
2 CONC nd is becoming dim. The first meal is announced. Here I have a first view of my fellow passengers, only fourteen in number. Immediately I scan them. First there is an elderly woman who talks most rapidly in Danish to the doctor se
2 CONC ated opposite. Next to her is a big bulky gentleman of about twenty. Then a Swedish lady. I know that by her appearance and then an elderly gentleman who sits by my side. I am on the right hand of the captain, the reason for which
2 CONC  I think is that he thought of me as a young girl travelling alone and he told my father that he would take very good care of me. Across from me is a young lady (I am only a girl). Next to her is a young married couple and then th
2 CONC e ship’s doctor. At the other table sits a young woman with two girls about ten and four and a small boy perhaps four. With them is another younger lady. So far we have a very Scandinavian family but here sit’s a Frenchman to wind
2 CONC  up the list. At the dinner in the evening the captain appears and he is a very nice gentleman at least from his manners and conversation at the table. I feel as though I had almost gotten into another country for the many waiters
2 CONC  and most of the passengers are Scandinavian. Oct. 11, 1907 Today I have become somewhat acquainted with the passengers and also the numerous doors, hallways etc. The old Danish lady appeared on deck in the afternoon and told us a
2 CONC n exceedingly fine method of finding our room. She tied a red ribbon to her door. If we all did that I think that we would have a mix-up. The old gentleman I find is in the Norwegian army, Lieutenant Colonel. He explained to me th
2 CONC e situation in Norway in regard to adopting the old true Norse language and poor ignorant I tried to learn my lesson well. The Frenchman asks me to play a game of schuffel-board (sic), He says he is married and I am most terribly 
2 CONC glad he is. Oh! Such a lovely day. We all know what a beautiful Indian summer day is on land and on the ocean is just as lovely if not more so. I think this pleasant beginning of my trip is very encouraging and I feel very happy. 
2 CONC Oct.12, Saturday I arose this morning at 6.30 and had a nice salt water bath and after an exhilarating walk was ready to enjoy my breakfast. We begin in true American style with fruit, cereal and light meat. Then there is an assor
2 CONC tment of all kinds of cold meats and quite a variety of cheeses. The lady across the table from me is from Mexico and tells of her life on the big ranch. I have come to the conclusion that the gentleman and lady next to her have b
2 CONC een recently married for those people are easily stamped and these come up to requirements. I said that I was not going to be seasick but wait a minute I feel a22 queer sensation in my throat and it seems to descend from there on.
2 CONC  Up I go on deck therefore but that ozone of yesterday seems to have vanished. I wonder why. A little Swedish boy appears on deck and between his little English and my small knowledge of the Norwegian language we get on finely and
2 CONC  soon have a game of schuffel-board in which he tries to cheat but I get after him, little rogue. Time to eat boullion and rolls. I really think I am doing fine as I come in for all meals. After dinner I open one of my packages wh
2 CONC ich my brother has kindly provided me with in order to please and amuse me. There is a mirror in this one and I think I answer the requirements of blue and orange with black under the eyes and yellow within. Well cheer up! As was 
2 CONC the inscription on the mirror. Oct. 13, Sunday Today is Sunday. We are awakened by the sweet strains of that grand and mighty Lutheran hymn “A mighty fortress is our God”. After it has left the music saloon in our cabin, the strai
2 CONC ns come floating back to us from different parts of our floating village where others are reminded of the almighty God and his special day. On deck it is beautiful. The sun comes beaming down and the water dances in its light. It 
2 CONC is the sunshine of God portraying the beauty and cheerfulness of his love. I am not forgetting exercise and deep breathing. The children and I have become very good friends and after visiting the candy boxes we have exercise in a 
2 CONC game of schuffel-board. Then we read “The Water Baby” and find him a real interesting little chap. This afternoon the Swedish lady who sits at my side of the table and who by the way told me she is a catholic, the old Danish lady,
2 CONC  the Frenchman and myself went into the smoking room to have afternoon tea and cakes. Mrs Danish lady is a great talker but has a great deal of education although she often times does not show her culture because she does not allo
2 CONC w one to get a word in edgewise if she wants to make a strong argument. Gradually we talked on religion - atheist (Frenchman) Unitarian (Danish lady), Catholic (Swedish lady), Lutheran (myself). The Danish lady and the Frenchman w
2 CONC ere the chief speakers while he was trying to refute her arguments in belief in God. He wanted proofs - the Devil’s weak stand against God. I hope that the atheist carried away some thoughts. This morning we were happy to see a st
2 CONC eamer behind us even if it was quite distant on the horizon. Soon we were quite astonished when our steamer turned around and started towards its friend for it seems to me that a steamer met on the lone sea ought to be friendly. I
2 CONC n some time we could see the other ship quite plainly and discerned that it was not moving and no smoke arose from it. We signalled out to it for it had signals out, but we went past it and then turned around. I learned afterwards
2 CONC  that the signals said according to international language that a man was overboard or they thought on our boat that was the trouble. If that was the case we of course could be of little assistance so we went our way. Oct. 16 Fog 
2 CONC we have had over Newfoundland banks. This time the rule that fog rules the banks of Newfoundland was not proved by the exception. The usual rising question of “Did you hear the fog horn last night?” - but as for me they did not pe
2 CONC netrate Slumberland. Last night the ship rocked considerably but I got up free from that dreaded feeling which land people are wont to experience. After breakfast I had a great desire for something good to read, especially as I wa
2 CONC s the only passenger ready to go up on deck. I feel an instinct (?) or is it the package I feel and I open one of the small tokens my brother has given me for today but has ascertained the time for four o clock (Well it’s past fou
2 CONC r o clock in the morning so it is all right!). In it is - a book and something which I am sure that I will enjoy for it is by Henry Van Dyke - “The Friendly Year”. Up I go on deck with a companion under my arm. It makes me feel so
2 CONC  good to read something comforting, uplifting and strengthening. He seems to be talking to me heart to heart when he says “Carry this little flower with you. It is not the bonniest blossom in Scotland, but it is the clearest for t
2 CONC he message that it will bring to you and you will remember that love is not getting, but giving; not a wild dream of pleasure and a madness of desire - oh no, love is not that - it is goodness and honour and peace and a pure livin
2 CONC g, yes, love is those; and it is the best thing in the world, and the thing that lives longest. And this is 23 what I am wishing you and yours with this bit of white heather”. It seems as if he had taken a flower that is also so d
2 CONC ear to old Norge and with this emblem and its meaning in my heart I will try to make the most out of my journey. I have read many enobling thoughts but I must stop for God’s world is so beautiful today. Across the rolling bluish-g
2 CONC rey and silver sea is a soft still light. To one in depressed spirits it might seem a gloomy light but to me it seems comforting and restful. At the edge of the horizon it is bright, the clouds here dark and there light, breaking 
2 CONC at places to let the blue sky and golden light peep through. There seems to be a world beyond filled with sunshine which lines the clouds with silver. Gradually the clouds seem to float away from the sea for lo! the sun is breakin
2 CONC g and now it casts its silvery light across the water over which nothing moves but occasionally a bird, the companion to a very lonely ship on the deep. Thus the day is heralded so peacefully, yet gloriously. Oct. 17 - 20 We have 
2 CONC had stormy weather but I have done my best. I have been at the table all but the 19th when I ate in my room but for supper I went visiting next door. It is glorious to see the elements in such a fury. Every day I have gone up on d
2 CONC eck for a few minutes at least to look out and also to get some good fresh air. Oct. 20 Today is a sunshiny Sunday. Most everybody is on deck for land is in sight. It is the rough and rugged coast of northern Scotland. A little ro
2 CONC wboat is a welcome sight after ten days on the lonely sea and I only wish it would come closer so we could see some truly Scotchmen. It is rather misty to the north so the Orkney Islands are very dim. This afternoon the deck stewa
2 CONC rd took a party of us around the steamer. First we went through the steerage class which really was quite clean and comfortable but considering what a passenger in that class must pay and what a cabin passenger pays I think that t
2 CONC hey do not get near enough comparatively. They are crowded and the air in their compartments is not so very good. On stormy days I think it must be very unpleasant down where they live in the low close part of the ship. We saw the
2 CONC  kitchen which was very clean excepting the floors which were slippery but considering they were smooth style and they probably hadn’t cleaned up the floor after that meal as they were still scouring pots it wasn’t bad. We didn’t 
2 CONC see them bake those good pastry cakes and the bread is baked after midnight. I believe many parts of the ship they do not take passengers into such as cold storage rooms, machinery rooms etc. The stewards and waiters live about si
2 CONC xteen in one large state room and in the middle is a long table upon which they can take their meals. They have the same food as the passengers they serve I believe. The stewardesses live on the same floor as most of the first and
2 CONC  second class passengers, in second class locality. The ship has a crew of over two hundred or between one and two hundred but where these are all stored is really remarkable. After we came back from our trip we tidied up consider
2 CONC ably for that night we had the captain’s dinner. The tables are bedecked with two large festive cakes on each. On the top is an officer on a throne bedecked with sugar roses and he is pompously driving a large white swan. Poor swa
2 CONC n he has a heavy load as the cake is covered with Norsk, Dansk, Swensk and Americansk banners and around the side the fancy paper canons which have a small likeness of our S.ship on. It makes it all the more cruel as he has only t
2 CONC he thin structure of napkins for his makeup etc. At each place is a banner to represent the nationality of the receiver. I received an American flag (banner) but afterward the captain gave me a Norsk one so I was fully represented
2 CONC . The Frenchman got an American flag for which he was not grateful but I am glad I didn’t get a French one. We have a very nice dinner and afterwards the captain gives me the gay officer on his gay pinnacle of cake and candy. Towa
2 CONC rd the last of the dinner the band plays the Scandinavian and the American nation songs. While the band is honouring United States I remember the little surprise package which my brother has given me to open tonight at dinner. I u
2 CONC nwrap it and there is a little silk stars and stripes. Quite a pleasant 24 coincidence. I show my colors and am very proud. Oct. 21 The last day on board. This morning I enjoyed the last of the ocean and left my packing and prepar
2 CONC ing till this afternoon. The Danish lady and I have become quite good friends during the trip. During the rough period she sent me a little piece of paper filled with lemon drops and afterward gave me some of her peppermint wafers
2 CONC . Today I went in to greet her and out of the kindness of her heart she gave me more bits of advice from preparing salt herring to exercise to develop my lungs. When she let me go it was lunch time and again after when I came to d
2 CONC o various things which she had told me to do I had to hurry to prepare my trunk in the baggage room and finish my letter home. After dinner she asked me to come into the saloon and she would teach me some excellent exercises which
2 CONC  I ought to do. However when I came in she had the Colonel in conversation and asked me to wait till ten o clock. At about ten o clock I appeared but she was not finished so I never received my instruction. After dinner we arrived
2 CONC  in Christians and about seven thirty. It was dark so we could only see a little of the land we were coming to. The lights of the little town sent out a welcome to us. When the tender came out, about fifty passengers got off. Whil
2 CONC e some freight was being unloaded which took quite a while many greetings and farewells were exchanged between friends which had been made, no doubt, on board ship Oct. 22 This morning we awake and can see out of the port holes th
2 CONC e picturesque coast of Christiania fjord. Everyone had packed their sea clothes except those on board for Kovenhavn and are all prepared and happy to stand on land again. We have quite a table full at breakfast. Some faces very un
2 CONC familiar so early in the day. We enter Christiania harbour There are three Russian battle ships. As our boat passes them our band plays the Russian national hymn in honor of which their ships are lined with men, erect and with cap
2 CONC s at their sides. It was quite an effective ceremony and I really felt very much of that spirit of international peace “When war shall cease and perfect peace shall over flow the land”. Now we near the dock and I really do not kno
2 CONC w whether to expect any one or not. Yes! There is an old friend of the families, Mr Hauge. I know he will give me a most hearty welcome, as for me, he is a friend in the truest sense. After bidding good bye to my steamship friends
2 CONC  I am soon down the stairs and am safe on my father’s fatherland. Mr Hauge and I go to the street-car and are soon passing Norway’s best in buildings. First the Storthinget, the Castle, the National Theatre and the Noble buildings
2 CONC , and the University. They are all very pleasant and of very good architecture, but are on a small plan comparatively as Norway is in population and wealth to many other countries. The flag waved over the castle, so the king was h
2 CONC ome (but I hadn’t time to call on him). It seems rather queer to come into a small city as Christiania where there are no buildings higher than ?? stories, and find a good share of the people living in apartments and comparatively
2 CONC  few houses. The people here dress much the same as home, and the hats with the big bows on are as much in evidence as on Main Street home. The conductors on the street cars are most diplomatic and officer-like men. They salute ea
2 CONC ch other most precisely and as for politeness they get off very often while you enter their car. We soon alight at our destination and I am soon heartily welcomed into a Norwegian home. Mrs Hauge, Ella (six) and Anna (two) are ver
2 CONC y kind to me and at once I am at home. Ella remembers me from America but has forgotten all her English as for Anna I have never seen her before but she comes to me and smiles and we are at once friends. Mr Hauge soon takes me to 
2 CONC his mother who lives in the next story. I now have to bring forth my fragmentary Norsk and I really get along quite well. Mr Hauge has such a lovely relationship to his mother, and that love which makes good and upright men if the
2 CONC ir mother is such. Mrs Gabriel Hauge is a very refined, lovely woman and her home shows her admirable taste and desire to follow in the footsteps of the Great Master. 25 For dinner, which the upper class in Norway has at half past
2 CONC  two, we have fine kjod suppe med gron sager (meat soup with vegetables in), roast veal, potatoes, sour cream gravy, cauliflower and fresh fruit. This was a fine beginning to my meals in Norway as all have good food here I believe
2 CONC . In the afternoon Mr Hauge came home again from his factory and we had coffee and cakes. Mrs Gabriel Hauge and Mr Hauge’s sister Dora came down again. They had been down for dinner. For supper about half past seven we have quite 
2 CONC an assortment of Norwegian rye bread and delicious French bread with poppy seeds in. Then many kinds of cheeses, meats etc. One very good cheese which we get in Norway is made out of the whey of goat’s milk and it is very deliciou
2 CONC s. The milk here tastes so good and I drink much of it. In the evening, Mrs Hauge’s brother, Mr Christensen comes and hilsene paa mi” (greets me). Afterward I go to bed in a truely Norwegian bed room with the bare floor covered wi
2 CONC th linoleum and very cold too. The sheets have a wide strip of hardanger embroidery on the top and the small pillow about the third the size of ours which is on top of one about three times as long as an ordinary American pillow h
2 CONC as also Hardanger embroidery. So I soon close my eyelids on Norsk handiwork and sleep pleasantly after my first day in Norway. Oct. 23 - 24 Today I went down town with Mrs Hauge and the stores I find are quite different from ours.
2 CONC  As we came down Johannes Karl Gaden the band was playing in the green about the theatre. Many people promenaded and I had a good chance to see Christiania’s style. Most of the people were in very good taste but I don’t think as c
2 CONC hic as New Yorkers by any means. In the stores the clerks are so very polite and obliging even if you do not make a purchase and that adds a great deal to the pleasure of shopping. There are no department stores like those which w
2 CONC e have in our cities but stores are smaller and certainly only one, two, or three kinds of goods generally. When tying up a bundle in a store a little string handle is almost always tied and one can carry many more bundles and not
2 CONC  loose them so easily, I believe, this way. In Christiania electricity and petroleum is used most for lighting, and I believe many homes use kerosene. Thursday I walk to the post office with Mr Hauge which is not far from the cent
2 CONC re of the town, and from there I go down town alone and walk around looking for the most part and making a few small purchases. I come across the market and there I enjoy myself. The fruit and vegetables in Norway are as a general
2 CONC  thing very fine but not such a great variety and scarcer than in America. However this year there has been so much rain that the fruit is very small and poor, and the market really looked poverty-stricken in the fruit places. The
2 CONC  flowers and plants on one side didn’t escape my attention long, and I was soon with a bunch of yellow flowers on one hand and a plant in the other. Mrs Hauge, Ella and I went to see Mr Hauges galvanising plant in the afternoon. I
2 CONC t is small but it is the first using that electrical method in Norway and there are very good prospects. October 25 I arose a little after five this morning and it was drizzling, a slow rain outside. After breakfast at a little pa
2 CONC st six, I was off again to get the train for Lillehammer. It was very interesting to ride through the streets in open cart and see that many were yet in Slumberland at a little before seven. The people in Norway believe in “Workin
2 CONC g to live” while the Americans “Live to work” as a Swedish gentleman who had lived a few years in America said. They certainly want a great deal of sleep and plenty of good food- After wishing me a good journey and welcoming me ba
2 CONC ck to Christiania, Mr Hauge left me in the train. The train was composed of first and second class coaches. Many people travel third class as it is cheaper and yet neat and quite comfortable. The train is divided into several comp
2 CONC artments. A small passage way goes along one side of the coach and off from this which has windows before every compartment are doors and inner windows that the passenger not only gets light from the side 26 without the passage bu
2 CONC t from the other side also. Along the walls of each compartment are wooden curved benches above which are shelfs to put luggage. I was alone in the women’s compartment for which I was quite thankful. It is a six hour journey to Li
2 CONC llehammer but considering the speed of Norwegian trains and the waits at small stations on the journey it was quite like one on the “Peanut Road” but for all that pleasant. I could not see much as there was quite a dense mist but 
2 CONC had plenty of time to reverie on what I had seen and what my aunt’s home and it surroundings would be like. At a few stations before we came to Lillehammer my aunt and a cousin came on. I had been looking for them as my aunt promi
2 CONC sed to meet me at a previous station. Tante was so sweet in her dark blue suit and little silk bonnet such as deaconesses at home use. On her arm and the pin on her white collar was the Red Cross emblem of the Society of Red Cross
2 CONC  nurses. Her gray hair was softly arranged under her little dark bonnet. Ellen had a simple and good appearance. She had not pushed herself forward as many girls do at only seventeen but was just a girl in simple cap and jacket. I
2 CONC t was very amusing our first conversation in broken Norwegian on my part and almost as bad English on their part. I was heartily welcomed to Lillehammer but then Tante told me I was to live with her doctor’s family instead of in h
2 CONC er convalescent home as I had expected. There she said it was a refined Christian home. I would be much better off and have better advantages than in a home with sick people and rather a unhome like atmosphere as such a place has.
2 CONC  So at the station Fru, Doctor Arentz met us. There was also my aunt’s helper, another Red Cross nurse and a patient who had really grown quite one of the family. They were very cordial in their greetings and as my cousin and I wa
2 CONC lked along together we didn’t seem very strange. Tante and Ellen came in the Dr’s home with me and after a while we had tea and cakes served very daintily on dainty blue and white ware with tea cloth to match. Soon I met the docto
2 CONC r, a man of about thirty nine and very nice appearance. Shortly after in walks a genuine little Norsk, girl, blue eyes, light hair and rosy cheeks. The name is Gudrun, but is always called Guri and I can tell you Guri and I were s
2 CONC oon friends. After Tante and Ellen had gone I went to my room to arrange my clothes. It is a very neat and cheerful bed room but really quite simple with its painted wooden walls and floor. Adjoining it is a room where my clothes 
2 CONC can hang and I can keep my trunk and suit case. Soon the other little girl, Agnes who is nine, three years older than Guri, comes in from school. Agnes is quite slender and has a mass of beautiful hair which hangs down her back wh
2 CONC ile Guri’s is short and straight. Our first meal, dinner at half past two tasted splendid. Pea soup, beef with prunes in, potatoes and sour cream gravy and I believe fruit with accessories was the menu. The afternoon I went up to 
2 CONC see my aunt’s home. It is only five to ten minutes’ walk from here, and just at the outskirts of the little town. It is simple and quite small but very neat and tasty. October 26 As I am getting up this morning the maid brings oat
2 CONC  meal gruel to me. I always thought of this for invalids but it tasted very good and quite a good many people use it here as breakfast is not before 9.30. For breakfast we have first a large dish of oat meal which we do not put su
2 CONC gar or milk on but drink milk with it. After we have chocolate, rolls, bread, cheeses, and cold meats. This we use every morning. Soon after breakfast I went out with my cousin to walk to a mt. falls up in the woods. It was so new
2 CONC  to me these beautiful mountains and as I walked up the rather steep but well-built path I was so anxious to get all the good possible from the beautiful surroundings God had placed here, the grand tall pines in their rocky bed, t
2 CONC he little brooks running through the dim woods, the bright green mosses and dull rocks and old autumn leaves and the path winding in among all these natural glories. After quite a little walk we came to the falls tumbling down the
2 CONC  rock terraces in the woods. It came tumbling down in torrents as there had been much rain previous. Oh! I felt such a desire to be able to portray some of that beauty in a painting for what is more beautiful to adorn the walls of
2 CONC  a home than pictures of beautiful scenes, and interesting bits of life skilfully portrayed.
