1 NAME Leif Andreas /Bryn/
2 GIVN Leif Andreas
2 _MARNM Bryn
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 11 MAY 1898
2 ADDR
3 CITY Sandefjord
3 STAE Vestfold
3 CTRY Norway
1 DEAT
2 DATE 18 FEB 1940
2 PLAC Fra Buenos Aires til Stavanger 
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} BRYN, LEIF ANDREAS, skipsfører, Sandefjord. Født 11. mai 1898 i Larvik, s. av Carl Ingemar Bryn, f. 1867 i Sandefjord, og Elisabeth f. Andersen, f. 1863 i Larvik. Gift 1921 i Norderhov med Thora Heieren, f. 1892 i 
2 CONC Norderhov. 1 barn. Styrmanns-, radio- og skipsførereksamen. Var kaptein på m/s Sangstad, og mistet livet 18. februar 1940 da skipet ble torpedert av en tysk ubåt øst for Kirkwall på Orknøyene, nord for Skottland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Torpedoed on Febr. 18-1940 by U-61 (Oesten) and sunk 59 03N 01 08E, when on a voyage from Buenos Aires to Stavanger with a cargo of grain. The captain died. Norway was still neutral at that time. Jan-Olof, Sweden has sent me a cop
2 CONC y of an old newspaper clipping, dated March 16-1940, relating some information from the subsequent inquiry at the consulate in Newcastle-on-Tyne on Febr. 28, at which time 1st Mate Olaf Wroldsen, 2nd Mate A. Huseby, 2nd Engineer W
2 CONC aldemar Martinsen, and 3rd Engineer Arne Melsom were questioned.
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1st mate presented a report which states that Sangstad had departed Buenos Aires on Jan 13 with a cargo of rye and wheat for the Norwegian State, properly marked as a neutral ship, with her flag lit up. Shortly after midnight 
2 CONC on Febr. 14, when about 230 n. miles west of the Faroe Islands she was stopped by a British warship. An officer and 5 soldiers were sent on board, whereupon she was ordered into Kirkwall where she arrived on Febr 16. After her car
2 CONC go and crew had been inspected she was free to leave in the afternoon of Febr. 17, setting a course for Hvidingsøy.
2 CONT
2 CONT At 05:10 on Febr. 18, when in the position given above, a tremendous explosion occurred on the port side of the ship, plunging them into darkness and causing water to come gushing into the engine room through the skylight. The 2nd
2 CONC  engineer was on watch and was able to stop the engine before running up. Officer on watch on the bridge at the time was the 2nd mate, who states that she was probably struck in the after part of No. 2 hatch. The 1st mate, asleep 
2 CONC in his cabin, was flung up to the ceiling. He grabbed his lifebelt and managed to force the cabin door open and get on deck. Sangstad immediately started to sink, listing heavily to port, making the launching of the starboard life
2 CONC boat very difficult (the port boat had been destroyed). While lowering the boat the entire boatdeck was suddenly lit up by the U-boat. As soon as the lifeboat was on the water, the line was cut and orders given to come aft, but as
2 CONC  the ship still had some movement forward the lifeboat drifted behind her, so 7 men had to jump overboard from the stern just before she sank. Among them was Captain Leif Bryn, who had come on the bridge right after the explosion 
2 CONC and was seen swimming towards the lifeboat. He was never found, but the other 6 were picked up by the lifeboat, while 6 were on a raft.
2 CONT
2 CONT At dawn an aircraft passed overhead, and shortly afterwards a U-boat was seen very close by. They rowed towards it while signalling in the hope of getting assistance but it continued north. About 3 hours later an aircraft circled 
2 CONC around several times, and that afternoon a trawler was seen, but did not appear to see their signals. After sundown, 2 destroyers were spotted and this time their signals were seen and one of them approached to pick them up at abo
2 CONC ut 17:45. They were landed on Febr. 21. The 6 on the raft had been picked up by another destroyer previously.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Rohwer gives the position as 59 00N 00 25E).
